{:edb/db-before-t 9028 :edb/db-after-t 9029 :edb/tx-data [[13194139542341 50 #inst "2026-10-28T01:41:57.020Z" 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186098187 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098191 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186099609 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099613 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186101142 1071 "Calculate the product $AI_{3}$ given that $I_{3}$ is the $3 \\cdot 3$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 false] [17592186101142 1071 "Calculate the product $AI_{3}$ given that $I_{3}$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186101142 1074 "Multiplying a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix leaves every entry unchanged. Both matrices are $3\\times3$, so the product is defined.\n\n$$\nAI_3=A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186101408 1074 "The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is the third row of $I_3$. Multiplying $B$ on the left by this row selects the third row of $B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186101962 1064 "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 false] [17592186101962 1064 "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 true] [17592186265864 1074 "Notice that $B$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542341 17592186576411 false] [17592186265864 1074 "Notice that $B$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\fr